<mn>45</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> in the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45 equals minus 12 m plus b\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math>, which can be rewritten as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45 plus 12 m equals b\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></math>. It's also given in the table that when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 6\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals 45\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>45</mn></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45\"><mn>45</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> in the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45 equals 6 m plus b\"><mn>45</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math>, which can be rewritten as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45 minus 6 m equals b\"><mn>45</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45 plus 12 m\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>m</mi></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi></math> in this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45 minus 6 m equals negative 45 plus 12 m\"><mn>45</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>m</mi></math>. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6 m\"><mn>6</mn><mi>m</mi></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45 equals negative 45 plus 18 m\"><mn>45</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><mi>m</mi></math>. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45\"><mn>45</mn></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"90 equals 18 m\"><mn>90</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mi>m</mi></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5 equals m\"><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"m equals 5\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"m\"><mi>m</mi></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 12\"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> in the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45 equals 5 left parenthesis negative 12 right parenthesis plus b\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45 equals negative 60 plus b\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math>. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60\"><mn>60</mn></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"15 equals b\"><mn>15</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></math>. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"m equals 5\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b equals 15\"><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"m\"><mi>m</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"15\"><mn>15</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi></math> in the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals 5 x plus 15\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></math>. Thus, the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals 5 x plus 15\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></math> represents the linear relationship between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math>. It’s also given that the graph of the linear equation representing this relationship passes through the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis one fourth comma a right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"one fourth\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> in the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals 5 x plus 15\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals 5 left parenthesis one fourth right parenthesis plus 15\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></math>, which is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals five fourths plus 15\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals StartFraction 65 Over 4 EndFraction\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>65</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. Note that 65/4 and 16.25 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017722,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"e25f0807","skill_cd":"H.B.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Linear functions","createDate":1755117017722,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":"","external_id":"b09c918e-97c2-4a8b-9406-9ed217790fb4","primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"0ae4a5b9-38d9-4e10-a06f-c3eb054f26ee","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<figure class=\"table\"><table border=\"1\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"width: 47.701%; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\"><math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math></th>\n<th style=\"width: 47.701%; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\"><math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 47.701%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"negative 12\"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 47.701%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"negative 45\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 47.701%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 47.701%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"45\"><mn>45</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></figure>\n<p>The table shows two values of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> and their corresponding values of <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math>. The graph of the linear equation representing this relationship passes through the point <math alttext=\"left parenthesis one fourth comma a right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>. What is the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["16.25","65/4"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 65 Over 4 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>65</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. The linear relationship between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> can be represented by the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"m\"><mi>m</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi></math> are constants. It's given in the table that when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals negative 12\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals negative 45\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 12\"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> in the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45 equals minus 12 m plus b\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math>, which can be rewritten as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45 plus 12 m equals b\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></math>. It's also given in the table that when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 6\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals 45\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>45</mn></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45\"><mn>45</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> in the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45 equals 6 m plus b\"><mn>45</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math>, which can be rewritten as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45 minus 6 m equals b\"><mn>45</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45 plus 12 m\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>m</mi></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi></math> in this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45 minus 6 m equals negative 45 plus 12 m\"><mn>45</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>m</mi></math>. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6 m\"><mn>6</mn><mi>m</mi></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45 equals negative 45 plus 18 m\"><mn>45</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><mi>m</mi></math>. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45\"><mn>45</mn></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"90 equals 18 m\"><mn>90</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mi>m</mi></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5 equals m\"><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"m equals 5\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"m\"><mi>m</mi></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 12\"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> in the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45 equals 5 left parenthesis negative 12 right parenthesis plus b\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 45 equals negative 60 plus b\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math>. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60\"><mn>60</mn></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"15 equals b\"><mn>15</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></math>. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"m equals 5\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b equals 15\"><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"m\"><mi>m</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"15\"><mn>15</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi></math> in the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals 5 x plus 15\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></math>. Thus, the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals 5 x plus 15\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></math> represents the linear relationship between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math>. It’s also given that the graph of the linear equation representing this relationship passes through the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis one fourth comma a right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"one fourth\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> in the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals 5 x plus 15\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals 5 left parenthesis one fourth right parenthesis plus 15\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></math>, which is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals five fourths plus 15\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals StartFraction 65 Over 4 EndFraction\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>65</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. Note that 65/4 and 16.25 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>","externalid":"b09c918e-97c2-4a8b-9406-9ed217790fb4","correct_answer":["16.25","65/4"]},"createDate":1755117017722,"updateDate":1755117017722}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017722, 1755117017722),
    ($SATQ$e2e3942f$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$022887-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Systems of two linear equations in two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<div class="stimulus_reference " xmlns="http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1"><p class="standalone_statement style:1 "><span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e2e3942f/img_001_87309bb9.png" alt="Equation 1: y equals, 2 x plus 1. Equation 2: y equals, a, x minus 8."></span></span></p></div>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">In the system of equations above, <span class="italic">a</span>&nbsp;is a constant. If the system of equations has no solution, what is the value&nbsp;of&nbsp;<span class="italic">a</span>&nbsp;?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e2e3942f/img_000_cf8b7131.png\" alt=\"negative one half\"></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p>0</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p>1</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>2</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. A system of two linear equations has no solution when the graphs of the equations have the same slope and different <span class="italic">y-</span>coordinates of the <span class="italic">y</span>-intercepts. Each of the given equations is written in the slope-intercept form of a linear equation, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e2e3942f/img_002_6e4ca2eb.png" alt="y equals, m x plus b"></span>, where <span class="italic">m</span> is the slope and <span class="italic">b</span> is the <span class="italic">y</span>-coordinate of the <span class="italic">y</span>-intercept of the graph of the equation. For these two linear equations, the <span class="italic">y-</span>coordinates of the <span class="italic">y</span>-intercepts are different: <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e2e3942f/img_003_259db25f.png" alt="1"></span> and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e2e3942f/img_004_db570df6.png" alt="negative 8"></span>. Thus, if the system of equations has no solution, the slopes of the two linear equations must be the same. The slope of the first linear equation is 2. Therefore, for the system of equations to have no solution, the value of <span class="italic">a</span> must be 2.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect and may result from conceptual and computational errors.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
