nly if <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e53870b6/img_004_6374fc88.png\" alt=\"k equals 5\"></span>. Therefore, if the given equation has infinitely many solutions, the value of <span class=\"italic\">k</span> is 5.</p>\n","answer_style":"SPR","section":"Math","image_count":5,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959621,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"e53870b6","skill_cd":"H.A.","score_band_range_cd":1,"skill_desc":"Linear equations in one variable","createDate":1691007959621,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":"027085-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"e3ab0e0b-9dc0-4eb8-ae61-5552c39b8189","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"027085-DC","section":"Math","body":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference \" xmlns=\"http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1\"><p class=\"standalone_statement style:1 \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"6 x plus k, equals, 6 x plus 5\"></span></span></p></div>\n","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">In the given equation, <span class=\"italic\">k</span> is a constant. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of <span class=\"italic\">k</span> ?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"SPR","rationale":"<p>The correct answer is 5. Subtracting <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"6 x\"></span> from both sides of the given equation gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"k equals 5\"></span>, so for any value of <span class=\"italic\">x</span>, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"6 x plus k, equals, 6 x plus 5 \"></span> if and only if <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"k equals 5\"></span>. Therefore, if the given equation has infinitely many solutions, the value of <span class=\"italic\">k</span> is 5.</p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959621,"updateDate":1691007959621,"correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale":true}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959621, 1691007959621),
    ($SATQ$e62cfe5f$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$070611-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear functions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">According to a model, the head width, in millimeters, of a worker bumblebee can be estimated by adding 0.6 to four times the body weight of the bee, in grams. According to the model, what would be the head width, in millimeters, of a worker bumblebee that has a body weight of 0.5 grams?</p>
$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["2.6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is 2.6. According to the model, the head width, in millimeters, of a worker bumblebee can be estimated by adding 0.6 to 4 times the body weight, in grams, of the bee. Let <span class="italic">x</span> represent the body weight, in grams, of a worker bumblebee and let <span class="italic">y</span> represent the head width, in millimeters. Translating the verbal description of the model into an equation yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e62cfe5f/img_000_50ee3795.png" alt="y equals, 0 point 6 plus 4 x"></span>. Substituting 0.5 grams for <span class="italic">x</span> in this equation yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e62cfe5f/img_001_38636b4d.png" alt="y equals, 0 point 6, plus 4 times 0 point 5"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e62cfe5f/img_002_d80d60b0.png" alt="y equals 2 point 6"></span>. Therefore, a worker bumblebee with a body weight of 0.5 grams has an estimated head width of 2.6 millimeters. Note that 2.6 and 13/5 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>
